-- CONTROL for `s8-xmod-reexport-alias-constrained`: BYTE-IDENTICAL but for the
-- importer SPELLING (SELECTIVE `lib.rsel.{f}` / bare `f 1`, not `as R` / `R.f 1`).
--
-- GROUND TRUTH, HAND-DERIVED IDENTICALLY: `f x = x + 1` instantiated at `Int`,
-- so `f 1` = 2.
--
-- THIS CONTROL DOES TWO JOBS, and the second is why it is not redundant:
--   1. It localises an alias-arm regression, exactly as the unconstrained pair's
--      control does.
--   2. IT IS THE ATTRIBUTION FOR THE SIBLING'S REJECTING `run` CELL. This
--      spelling was ALWAYS green on `build` (it never had the scheme-env
--      defect), yet its `run` panics `intToString: not an Int` in precisely the
--      same way. One spelling broken and one spelling green on `build`, BOTH
--      broken identically on `run`, is what shows the run panic rides a
--      DIFFERENT channel from the build failure -- the #1369/#1425 dict-arity
--      seam, not the scheme-environment identity fix. Without this row the
--      sibling's REJECT could be misread as "the alias fix was incomplete".
--      MEASURED, not argued: on the fixed binary this file's cells are
--      check 0 / run 1 (`E-PANIC: intToString: not an Int`, 0 bytes stdout) /
--      build 0 / executed binary 0 printing `2` -- cell-for-cell IDENTICAL to
--      the alias sibling. The spelling changes nothing about the run arm.
import lib.rsel.{f}

main = println (f 1)
